        "id": 1002751
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-21T20:32:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we will always be finding under $PWD, why convert to an absolute path?",
      "revId": "e04f15a442a1a498f7095cbcb13a448f61cc5496",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "835f4d85_33d72f4f",
        "filename": "finder/finder_api.go",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 153,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137668
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-07-22T02:29:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The real reason is that I didn\u0027t want to think about the meaning of relative file paths.\n\nNow that I think about it, these are the reasons I\u0027ve come up with:\n\nUsing absolute paths helps if we want to provide a shell command. If a user issues the same relative-path command from two different directories using the same db, then the fact that the db stores absolute file paths allows it to disambiguate them.\n\nAdditionally, if the caller is a Go program that changes the working directory while FindNamed is running, then we wouldn\u0027t want the meaning of the filepaths to suddenly change.\n\nI also imagine that it would be easier for humans to debug a cache file where all of the entries are absolute file paths rather than relative.\n\nIn practice I suspect that these reasons aren\u0027t very important, but my performance testing (to my surprise) indicates that the cost of using long file paths everywhere is smaller than my measurement error, so I figure we might as well leave these benefits in place.",
      "parentUuid": "07ae267c_31d8bb94",
      "revId": "e04f15a442a1a498f7095cbcb13a448f61cc5496",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
